{"artifact_hash": "e84fa92addca501671a3a4af7dc9fb5eec68eadf3ab1d9e764ee7dccec2666bb", "artifact_ref": "local-teacher-2.json", "attempt": 2, "evidence": {"parsed": {"teacher_diagnosis": "Teacher json fence was not valid JSON: Unterminated string starting at", "teacher_parse_status": "failed"}, "raw": {"content": "```json\n{\n  \"failure_classification\": \"model_capability_insufficient\",\n  \"teacher_diagnosis\": \"The 1.7B worker (Qwen3-1.7B-Q4_K_M) cannot perform the required multi-hop lookup chain: (1) read the fallback string from os.environ.get() in Excerpt C, (2) use that string as a key into the DEFAULT_WORKERS dict in Excerpt B, (3) extract the model value, (4) compare against the size claim in Excerpt A. Across all three attempts the model pattern-matches on the most visually prominent model (Llama-3.3-70B) and assigns it to both slots, ignoring the actual slot-name-to-model mapping. Attempts 1 and 2 (identical prompt) produced byte-identical wrong output, confirming a deterministic capability gap rather than stochastic noise. Attempt 3 used a teacher-scaffolded prompt (added step-by-step instructions) and partially improved (got the boolean comparisons correct) but still failed on the slot names and model strings, confirming the model cannot reliably execute the lookup even with scaffolding. The 1.7B model is below the capability threshold for this task.\",\n  \"candidate_prompt_patch\": \"Replace the final instruction block (starting at 'Answer these eight questions') with the following decomposed sub-task sequence:\\n\\nSTEP 1: In Excerpt C, find the line containing ZTH_CAPABILITY_WORKER_NAME. The second argument to os.environ.get() on that line is a string in quotes. That string is the default worker slot name. Write it down.\\n\\nSTEP 2: In Excerpt C, find the line containing ZTH_CAPABILITY_TEACHER_NAME. The second argument to os.environ.get() on that line is a string in quotes. That string is the default teacher slot name. Write it down.\\n\\nSTEP 3: In Excerpt B, find the dictionary entry whose key matches the worker slot name from Step 1. Copy the value of its \\\"model\\\" field exactly, character for character. That is the default worker model.\\n\\nSTEP 4: In Excerpt B, find the dictionary entry whose key matches the teacher slot name from Step 2. Copy the value of its \\\"model\\\" field exactly, character for character. That is the default teacher model.\\n\\nSTEP 5: Excerpt A says the worker is 'normally the local 1.7B endpoint'. Does the default worker model from Step 3 contain the substring '1.7B'? If yes, answer true; if no, answer false.\\n\\nSTEP 6: Excerpt A says the local teacher is 'normally the 30B endpoint'. Does the default teacher model from Step 4 contain the substring '30B'? If yes, answer true; if no, answer false.\\n\\nSTEP 7: Scan every \\\"model\\\" value in Excerpt B. Does any of them contain the substring '1.7B'? If yes, answer true; if no, answer false.\\n\\nSTEP 8: Scan every \\\"model\\\" value in Excerpt B. Does any of them contain the substring '30B'? If yes, answer true; if no, answer false.\\n\\nNow output exactly one JSON object with these eight keys, using the values you derived above:\\nworker_default_matches_doc_1_7b_claim, teacher_default_matches_doc_30b_claim, one_point_7b_model_in_table, thirty_b_model_in_table, default_worker_slot, default_teacher_slot, default_worker_model, default_teacher_model.\\n\",\n  \"retry_guidance\": \"The decomposed step-by-step patch above should be applied to the prompt and retried up to 2 additional times. If both retries still fail, escalate by switching the worker to the 3B router model (Qwen/Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct-GGUF:Q4_K_M) or the 7B handoff model (Qwen/Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct-GGUF:Q4_K_M), which have sufficient capacity for multi-hop table lookups. Do not attempt more than 2 retries with the patched prompt on the 1.7B model.\",\n  \"corrected_reference_output\": {\n    \"worker_default_matches_doc_1_7b_claim\": false,\n    \"teacher_default